ellor of Lithuania.", "newValue": "Mikołaj III Radziwiłł (fl. 1515–1521) was a pivotal ecclesiastical figure in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, serving as Bishop of Samogitia during a period of significant religious and political consolidation. As a scion of the immensely powerful Radziwiłł family, his tenure exemplified the close intertwining of aristocratic power, state administration, and Church authority in the burgeoning Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. His father, Mikołaj Radziwiłł the Old, held the prestigious office of Grand Chancellor of Lithuania, establishing a dynastic precedent for influence that Mikołaj III continued within the Church hierarchy.

During his episcopacy from 1515 to 1521, Mikołaj III Radziwiłł oversaw the spiritual administration of Samogitia, a historically distinct region within the Grand Duchy that had only fully embraced Christianity relatively recently. His role involved strengthening the Catholic Church's institutional presence, promoting religious education, and managing diocesan affairs amidst a complex ethnic and political landscape. This period predated the full impact of the Protestant Reformation in the region, allowing for a focus on internal consolidation and the assertion of the Church's temporal and spiritual influence in a frontier territory.

