hough brief, exemplifies the Radziwiłł family's strategic expansion of influence through both secular and spiritual means.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-27T07:22:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30245631459", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Mikolaj Radziwiłł was the Bishop of Samogitia of Radziwill Family. He was son of Mikolaj Radziwiłł, Grand Chancellor of Lithuania.", "newValue": "Mikołaj Radziwiłł (II), born in 1492, emerged as a prominent ecclesiastical figure in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, serving as the Bishop of Samogitia from 1515 until his death in 1521. A scion of the powerful and increasingly influential Radziwiłł magnate family, his career was deeply intertwined with the political and religious landscape of the Jagiellonian era. His lineage, as the son of Mikołaj Radziwiłł the Elder (Grand Chancellor of Lithuania and Vilnius Voivode), positioned him at the nexus of secular power and spiritual authority, typical for high-ranking clergy from aristocratic families in the Commonwealth.

During his tenure as Bishop of Samogitia (modern-day western Lithuania), Mikołaj II oversaw a diocese that was a crucial bulwark against pagan traditions that persisted in the region and a strategic borderland with the Teutonic Order. While his episcopacy was relatively short, lasting only six years, it coincided with a period of intensified efforts by the Polish-Lithuanian state to consolidate Catholic influence and administrative control over its peripheral territories. His appointment, likely influenced by his father's considerable political clout, exemplifies the system of royal patronage that shaped the ecclesiastical hierarchy in the Grand Duchy.

